WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.569 --> 00:00:09.320
Health care in America is expensive in
part because consumers do not play the

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.610
same role that they do in other sectors.
Prices are high, and patients get many

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:17.720
services that will not improve their
health. Consumers will be able to shop

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:22.040
for non-emergency medical care only when
it is easy to compare prices on an

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.580
apples-to-apples basis. In today's
complex and opaque market, that's not

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:29.660
possible. The federal government is the
only entity with the reach and capacity to

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:35.329
unlock pricing for consumers. That will
require two steps: First, the government

00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:39.649
should establish a required pricing list
for common interventions. The key is

00:00:39.649 --> 00:00:44.600
standardization to ensure comparability
across competing suppliers. Second,

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.899
Congress should require insurance plans
to pay for all out-of-network services

00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:53.239
on the pricing list at their average
payments for those services. Consumers

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:56.120
would get to keep the savings when
getting care with prices below the

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:00.289
amounts paid by the insurers. These steps
would make it easy for consumers to shop

00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:05.280
for services and thus would encourage
providers to set their prices as low as possible.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.440
Is it possible for consumers to
shop for medical services under the

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.810
right circumstances? Let us know in our
poll. Also, let us know what other topics

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.950
you'd like our scholars to cover in 60
seconds, and be sure to like and

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:23.500
subscribe for more research and videos
from AEI.

